RPi::WiringPi test platform — docs/test-platform/ guide

Everything in this directory describes the unit-test hardware platform and is
GENERATED from a single source of truth. Do not hand-edit the generated files;
edit the source and regenerate (see below).

The full technical reference — the pin map, buses, loop-backs, default pin
states, collisions and power rails — is test-pinout-doc.md. This README is only
the regeneration runbook and a manifest of what each file is. It deliberately
carries NO pin data, so it cannot drift.

# Source of truth

  scripts/helpers/board-model.py        THE canonical electrical model
                                        (COMPONENTS / NETS / J1FUNC / DRIVER /
                                        POWER / SHEETS). Edit this to change the
                                        board; the schematic, KiCad project,
                                        netlist and pin doc all render from it.

  scripts/helpers/model-from-tests.py   an INDEPENDENT re-derivation of the same
                                        model from the test suite. It is diffed
                                        against board-model.py as a drift gate -
                                        if they disagree, one is stale.

  docs/test-platform/test-pinout-doc.tmpl.md
                                        the pin doc's prose/art template. Its one
                                        generated block - the section 12 Pi 5
                                        default-state table - comes from
                                        t/RPiTest.pm. Edit the template (not the
                                        rendered .md) for prose changes.

# Regenerate

The schematic Python venv is built automatically on first run; you also need
`netlistsvg` (Node) for the schematic PDF. Then:

    perl scripts/gen-test-platform.pl

It runs the Python generators in a throwaway scratch tree and files the
artifacts into this directory: the pinout JPEGs, the netlist, the multi-page
schematic PDFs, and the rendered pin doc. The wire-routed SVGs are scratch
intermediates (they feed the PDF) and are not kept. It never writes to the
repo's t/, nor to kicad/ - the KiCad board projects are scaffolded separately
(see "KiCad boards" below).

# Drift gate (runs under `make test`)

t/04-test-platform-model.t fails the build when:
  - model-from-tests.py has drifted from board-model.py,
  - any populated, non-frozen KiCad board project no longer validates,
  - a blessed board changed without a re-bless (check-board-locks.py),
  - a board that still has a model no longer implements it pin-for-pin
    (check-board-nets.py),
  - a board model disagrees with the IC datasheets - ALL-STOP
    (check-datasheets.py), or
  - test-pinout-doc.md is stale versus its template + sources.

Run the checks directly:
    python3 scripts/helpers/check-model-drift.py
    python3 scripts/helpers/check-board-locks.py
    python3 scripts/helpers/check-board-nets.py
    python3 scripts/helpers/check-datasheets.py
    python3 scripts/helpers/render-doc.py --check

# KiCad boards

The unit-test platform is five separate PCBs, each a self-contained KiCad
project in its own subdirectory of kicad/:

  kicad/rpi-wiringpi-unit-test-platform-board-1/  board 1 (Pi host, planned last)
  kicad/rpi-wiringpi-unit-test-platform-board-2/  board 2 (finalized & ordered)
  kicad/rpi-wiringpi-unit-test-platform-board-3/  board 3 (finalized & ordered)
  kicad/rpi-wiringpi-unit-test-platform-board-4/  board 4 (finalized & ordered)
  kicad/rpi-wiringpi-unit-test-platform-board-5/  board 5 (being hand-finalized)

These are NOT regenerated by gen-test-platform.pl. Each board is scaffolded
ONCE with gen-kicad.py, then hand-finalized in KiCad:

    python3 scripts/helpers/gen-kicad.py docs/test-platform/kicad/<board-dir> \
        [--model scripts/helpers/board-N-model.py]

That writes a stand-alone <board>.kicad_sch / .kicad_pro / fp-lib-table /
<board>.pretty into the directory (project name defaults to the directory's
basename; --model selects a per-board model, default board-model.py). It refuses
to overwrite an existing project, so a hand-edited board is never clobbered. The
drift gate validates every populated, non-frozen board; empty placeholders (no
.kicad_sch yet) are skipped.

Once a board is hand-finalized it becomes the source of truth for itself: it is
"blessed" (check-board-locks.py --bless <board-dir>) to freeze every design file
byte-for-byte, and listed in %FROZEN in t/04-test-platform-model.t so the
KiCad-validation step leaves it alone. Boards 2, 3 and 4 are finalized this way
and have NO generated model (they are authoritative as their own KiCad files);
board 5 is still being hand-finalized in KiCad, and gets blessed when done.

# Manifest (what each entry is, and what produces it)

  README                          this guide                         (hand)
  test-pinout-doc.md              full pin/wiring reference          <- render-doc.py
  test-pinout-doc.tmpl.md         its source template                (hand + generated)
  test-pinout-overview.jpg        bus/block pinout image             <- gen-pinout-images.py
  test-pinout-detail.jpg          pin-by-pin pinout image            <- gen-pinout-images.py
  test-platform-schematic-A3.pdf  multi-page schematic (A3, print)   <- gen-schematic.py -> netlistsvg -> gen-pdf.py
  test-platform-schematic-A4.pdf  multi-page schematic (A4)          <- (same chain)
  kicad/                          per-board KiCad projects, scaffolded <- gen-kicad.py
                                  once then hand-managed (see "KiCad      (one-shot)
                                  boards" above)
